<header class="main-header">
  <div class="series-title">Likutay Halachos &middot; Choshen Mishpat</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Da&rsquo;yonim 2</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Laws of Judges &mdash; 2</div>
</header>

<!-- ====== OPENING VERSE BLOCK ====== -->
<div class="verse-block">
  <em>Dinay momonos bishlosha</em> &mdash; <strong>&ldquo;Monetary cases [are adjudicated] by three [judges]&rdquo;</strong>
  <span class="source-ref">Based on the teaching beginning &ldquo;<span class="aramaic">T&rsquo;homos y&rsquo;chasyumu</span>&rdquo; &mdash; see there, Likutay [Moharan] I, Siman 9, on the verse &ldquo;A <em>tzohar</em> you shall make for the <em>taivah</em>, and to a cubit you shall finish it from above etc.&rdquo;</span>
</div>

<!-- ====== OS ALEPH ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 1</div>

<p>
The matter of <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;yonim</span> <span class="key-term-def">[judges]</span> according to what Rabbainu, <em>zecher tzadik livrachah</em> <span class="key-term-def">[may the memory of the righteous be for a blessing]</span>, wrote in the teaching that begins <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;T&rsquo;homos y&rsquo;chasyumu&rdquo;</span> in Siman 9, Likutay [Moharan] I, on the verse, <span class="scripture">&ldquo;A <em>tzohar</em> [light/window] you shall make for the <em>taivah</em> [ark], and to a cubit you shall finish it from above etc.&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 6:16)</span>: And the general principle that emerges from there [is] that when a person stands to pray, and then the darkness and the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> <span class="key-term-def">[husks/shells of impurity]</span> surround him and confuse him, then the essential counsel and remedy for this is to see to it that he speaks a <span class="key-term">dibbur emes</span> <span class="key-term-def">[a word/utterance of truth]</span>. And the truth is the light of Hashem Yisborach Himself, <span class="key-term">kivyachol</span> <span class="key-term-def">[as it were]</span>. And through this, Hashem Yisborach Himself, Who is the very essence of truth, <em>kivyachol</em>, illuminates for him. And then he sees the openings &mdash; how to exit from the darkness that surrounds him, etc. See there this matter well, for it is a very great principle in the service of the Creator Yisborach, in every place that one may be, as is explained there in the words of Rabbainu further on in another place, Siman 112, regarding this matter well. And <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillah</span> <span class="key-term-def">[prayer]</span> is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> <span class="key-term-def">[faith]</span> etc., see there.
</p>

<!-- ====== OS BAIS ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 2</div>

<p>
And behold, these two litigants who come to judgment and contradict one another &mdash; and one of them is certainly claiming falsely &mdash; this is the aspect of a blemish of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> [faith]. For he should have conducted his business dealings with <span class="key-term">emunah</span> [faithfulness/integrity], as they said <span class="source">(Shabbos 31a)</span>: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Did you conduct your business dealings with faithfulness?&rdquo;</span> And this one denies his fellow and claims falsely and has no trustworthiness &mdash; it emerges that at the time they come to judgment it is the aspect of a blemish of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>. And it is necessary to rectify and clarify the <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, and to clarify the truth &mdash; with whom [it lies]. However, it is impossible for flesh and blood to know with whom the truth lies, for the falsehood conceals the truth, for it is difficult to discern between the one who claims truth and the one who claims falsely. And what shall the judge do to bring out the judgment to its truth?
</p>

<p>
The counsel and remedy for this is the aforementioned matter that was stated regarding <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillah</span> [prayer], which is also in the aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> &mdash; namely, that the judge must see to it that he speaks a <span class="key-term">dibbur emes</span> [word of truth]. That is to say, that the judgment which he wishes to render upon the litigants should be in truth, without any inclination to any side, as it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;You shall not incline judgment&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 16:19)</span>, and as the Torah and our Sages of blessed memory have warned regarding this many times, for this is the essential thing. And through the judge being exceedingly careful in this, and all his words being only in truth &mdash; according to what appears to the eyes of his intellect as the truth, in accordance with the law of our holy Torah, or in accordance with his judgment if he sees that it is a fraudulent case, as is explained in the <span class="key-term">Shulchan Aruch</span> &mdash; then, when all his words are only in truth and he does not incline to any side, then it is fulfilled: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;G-d stands in the congregation of the Mighty; in the midst [of the judges He judges]&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 82:1)</span>. For then Hashem Yisborach stands there in the congregation of the upright judges whose words are in truth, for truth is the light of Hashem Yisborach Himself, <em>kivyachol</em>. And the essential longing of Hashem Yisborach is only for the truth.
</p>

<p>
And then the word of truth, which is the light of Hashem Yisborach, <em>kivyachol</em>, illuminates for the judge. And then he merits to see the truth &mdash; with which of the litigants [it lies], and he merits that the judgment becomes clarified for him to its truth. For the word of truth of the judge illuminates and reveals the truth from within the darkness and the falsehood of the opposing party who claims falsely.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;one who judges a judgment of truth to its truth&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Shabbos 10a)</span>, regarding which the commentators were precise about the word <strong>&ldquo;to its truth&rdquo;</strong> [<em>la&rsquo;amiso</em> &mdash; seemingly redundant after &ldquo;a judgment of truth&rdquo;]. But according to the above it is well-precisely fitting. <span class="precise">&ldquo;To its truth,&rdquo; specifically</span> &mdash; for it can certainly be that the judge will judge a judgment of truth according to the Torah based on their claims, and even so he may obligate the one who is actually innocent according to the truth. But in truth, when the judge is exceedingly careful that all his words should be in truth without any inclination, as above, then the truth, which is the light of Hashem Yisborach Himself, <em>kivyachol</em>, illuminates for him. And he merits to see the truth <strong>to its truth</strong> [<em>la&rsquo;amiso</em>], for the light is revealed from within the darkness through the word of truth, which is the aspect of the light of Hashem Yisborach Himself, <em>kivyachol</em>, as above. And understand this well. And this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;one who judges a judgment of truth <em>to its truth</em>&rdquo;</span> &mdash; <span class="precise">&ldquo;to its truth,&rdquo; specifically</span>, as above.
</p>

<div class="word-note">
<strong>Word correlation:</strong> The Hebrew phrase <em>din emes la&rsquo;amiso</em> (דין אמת לאמיתו) is analyzed here: <em>emes</em> = truth of Torah law as applied to the claims; <em>la&rsquo;amiso</em> = to its [deeper] truth, i.e. revealing who is actually truthful. The seemingly redundant word <em>la&rsquo;amiso</em> is explained as the higher level &mdash; the Divine light of truth itself revealing the hidden reality, beyond what formal legal analysis alone can discern.
</div>

<!-- ====== OS GIMMEL ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 3</div>

<p>
And this is what our Sages, of blessed memory, said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Whoever judges a judgment of truth to its truth becomes as if he became a partner with the Holy One, Blessed is He, in the work of Creation&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Shabbos 10a)</span>, as it says: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And it was evening and it was morning, one day&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 1:5)</span>. And there [in the context of Moshe judging] it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And all the people stood over you etc.&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 18:13&ndash;14)</span>. For it is brought there in the words of Rabbainu mentioned above, that through the word of truth mentioned above &mdash; through which one merits to pray &mdash; through this he sustains all the three worlds, which are the <span class="key-term">olam hashofel</span> <span class="key-term-def">[lower world]</span> etc. And therefore the judge who judges a judgment of truth to its truth, as above &mdash; which is also the rectification of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> [faith], which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillah</span> [prayer], as above &mdash; he too sustains all the three worlds, as above. And therefore he becomes a partner with the Holy One, Blessed is He, in the work of Creation, as above.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of <strong>three</strong> judges, for the three judges correspond to the aspect of the three lines of truth. For <span class="key-term">emes</span> <span class="key-term-def">[truth]</span> is comprised of three aspects: <span class="key-term">rosh</span>, <span class="key-term">soch</span>, <span class="key-term">sof</span> <span class="key-term-def">[beginning, middle, end]</span>, as is known &mdash; the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yaakov</span> who is comprised of the three <span class="key-term">Avos</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Patriarchs]</span>, who is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emes</span> [truth], and he is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> [judgment], as is brought in another place in Siman 2.
</p>

<div class="word-note">
<strong>Word correlation:</strong> The word <em>emes</em> (אמ&rdquo;ת) in Hebrew is composed of the letters <em>aleph</em>, <em>mem</em>, <em>sav</em> &mdash; the first, middle, and last letters of the <em>aleph-bais</em>. Hence it intrinsically embodies the three-fold structure of beginning (<em>rosh</em>), middle (<em>soch</em>), and end (<em>sof</em>), paralleling the three Patriarchs and the three judges.
</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of three judges, for the essential rectification of judgment depends upon truth &mdash; through which the truth is revealed to its truth. That is, they merit &mdash; through their words being in truth &mdash; that it becomes revealed who claims truth and who claims falsely, for the truth illuminates etc., as above. Also, the three judges who correspond to the three lines of truth hint that they sustain the three worlds that are explained in the aforementioned teaching, whose essential sustenance is through the rectification of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillah</span>, which is accomplished through truth. And this is also the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;a judgment of truth to its truth,&rdquo;</span> which is also the aspect of the rectification of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, as above.
</p>

<!-- ====== OS DALED ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 4</div>

<p>
And this is what is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And a bribe you shall not take, for the bribe will blind etc.&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 23:8)</span> &mdash; <span class="precise">&ldquo;will blind,&rdquo; specifically</span>. For the essential rectification of judgment is that the truth illuminates for him and he sees how to reveal the truth from within the darkness. But when he takes a bribe, it emerges that he has no truth. And even if he does not wish to incline the judgment, nevertheless, since he accepted a bribe, in any case he has no truth. And since he has no truth, then he remains blind, as is explained there in the words of Rabbainu mentioned above, for it is impossible to see except through the truth, which is the aspect of light, as above.
</p>

<!-- ====== OS HAY ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 5</div>

<p>
And therefore our Sages, of blessed memory, said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;One does not judge at night, only during the day&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Sanhedrin 34b)</span>. For day and night are the aspects of <span class="key-term">emes</span> [truth] and <span class="key-term">emunah</span> [faith], as is known. And the essential hold of the <span class="key-term">sheker</span> <span class="key-term-def">[falsehood]</span> and the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achara</span> <span class="key-term-def">[the Other Side / forces of impurity]</span> is in the aspect of night, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>. And therefore at night the <span class="key-term">emunah</span> is in a state of smallness, and it grows in the light of day, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Lamentations 3:23)</span>, as is brought in another place (Siman 35). For the essential rectification of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> is through <span class="key-term">emes</span> [truth], as Rabbainu wrote in the aforementioned teaching: <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;<em>Tzedek</em> [righteousness/justice], when it is joined with <em>emes</em> [truth], becomes <em>emunah</em> [faith].&rdquo;</span> See there.
</p>

<p>
And therefore judgment [takes place] during the day, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emes</span> [truth], through which one elevates and rectifies <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, as above. And then a unification of <span class="key-term">emes</span> and <span class="key-term">emunah</span> is made &mdash; which are the aspects of evening and morning, the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And it was evening and it was morning, one day&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 1:5)</span>. And this is accomplished through a judgment of truth, regarding which it is also said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And all the people stood over you from evening until morning&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 18:13&ndash;14)</span>, as above. For the essence of judgment and the court is to rectify the fallen <span class="key-term">emunah</span> that was blemished among these litigants, for one of them certainly does not conduct his business dealings with faithfulness. And through the judgment of truth, one elevates and rectifies <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, as above, in the aspect of <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;<em>Tzedek</em>, when it is joined with <em>emes</em>, becomes <em>emunah</em>.&rdquo;</span> And a unification of truth and faith, evening and morning, is made, as above.
</p>

<p>
And therefore the essential judgment is during the day, as it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And render justice in the morning&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Jeremiah 21:12)</span>, for then the truth illuminates, through which the <span class="key-term">emunah</span> is rectified and grows, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness&rdquo;</span>. And this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And they shall judge the people with righteous judgment&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 16:18)</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;<em>Tzedek</em>, when it is joined with <em>emes</em> etc.,&rdquo;</span> for <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> [judgment] is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emes</span> [truth], as it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;The judgments of Hashem are truth&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 19:10)</span>, as is brought in the words of Rabbainu, of blessed memory. And this is [the meaning of] <span class="scripture">&ldquo;righteous judgment&rdquo;</span> [<em>mishpat tzedek</em>] &mdash; the aspect of <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;<em>Tzedek</em>, when it is joined with <em>emes</em>, becomes <em>emunah</em>,&rdquo;</span> for this is the essential [purpose of the] sitting of the judges &mdash; to rectify the <span class="key-term">emunah</span> through the truth, that <span class="key-term">tzedek</span> and <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, truth and faith, should be joined together, as above.
</p>

<div class="word-note">
<strong>Root connection:</strong> The Aramaic formula <em>tzedek kad is&rsquo;chabrass b&rsquo;emes is&rsquo;avidass emunah</em> (from the Zohar) is the conceptual backbone of this entire halacha. <em>Tzedek</em> (justice/righteousness) = the feminine / <em>emunah</em> aspect; <em>emes</em> (truth) = the light that rectifies it. When the judge&rsquo;s words are in <em>emes</em>, the fallen <em>tzedek</em>/<em>emunah</em> is restored.
</div>

<!-- ====== OS VOV ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 6</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of why the upright judge is called <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Elokim</em>&rdquo;</span> [lit. G-d / judges], as it is written many times in the Torah: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;To the <em>Elokim</em> the matter of both of them shall come&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 22:8)</span>, and the like in many places. For the judge who judges a judgment of truth, and all his words are in truth &mdash; the light of Hashem Yisborach Himself, <em>kivyachol</em>, becomes clothed within him, as above, and as is well-explained in the aforementioned teaching. And therefore the judge himself is called &ldquo;<em>Elokim</em>,&rdquo; for the light of Hashem Yisborach became clothed within him, <em>kivyachol</em>, through the truth, which is His light Yisborach Himself, <em>kivyachol</em>, as above.
</p>

<!-- ====== OS ZAYIN ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 7</div>

<p>
And therefore the essential judges from the Torah are only <span class="key-term">s&rsquo;muchin</span> <span class="key-term-def">[ordained judges, holding an unbroken chain of ordination]</span> in <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisroel</span> <span class="key-term-def">[the Land of Israel]</span>. For <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisroel</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, as is explained in the aforementioned teaching. And the essential [purpose of] the judges is for the rectification of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, as above.
</p>

<!-- ====== OS CHES ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 8</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;A court does not concern itself [with a dispute] for less than the value of a <em>p&rsquo;rutah</em> [the smallest coin]&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Sanhedrin 8a)</span>. <span class="key-term">P&rsquo;rutah</span> alludes to <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillah</span> [prayer], as is explained in the aforementioned teaching regarding the saying of our Sages, of blessed memory: <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;<em>Uf&rsquo;ritah sif&rsquo;vasayh</em>&rdquo;</span> <span class="key-term-def">[&ldquo;and her lips move/separate&rdquo; &mdash; said of Channah&rsquo;s prayer]</span>.
</p>

<div class="word-note">
<strong>Word-play:</strong> <em>P&rsquo;rutah</em> (פרוטה, a small coin) is linked to the root <em>p-r-t</em> (פרט), connected to <em>uf&rsquo;ritah sif&rsquo;vasayh</em> (ופריטא שפוותיה, &ldquo;and her lips separated/moved&rdquo;), the description of Channah&rsquo;s silent prayer <span class="source">(I Samuel 1:13)</span>. Thus the &ldquo;p&rsquo;rutah&rdquo; is not merely a monetary unit but an allusion to the whispered prayer of the lips &mdash; which is itself the aspect of <em>emunah</em>.
</div>

<p>
And therefore <span class="key-term">p&rsquo;rutah</span> is the minimum measure of money in every context, for the essential <span class="key-term">emunah</span> depends upon money, as it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And faithfulness exceedingly&rdquo;</span> [<em>v&rsquo;emunah m&rsquo;od</em>] <span class="source">(Psalms 119:138)</span> &mdash; <strong>&ldquo;<em>m&rsquo;od</em>&rdquo; [exceedingly] refers to money</strong>, as Rabbainu of blessed memory wrote in another place, Siman 23. And therefore, when the money has the value of a <span class="key-term">p&rsquo;rutah</span>, then it has a hold in the aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillah</span>, which is also the aspect of <span class="aramaic"><em>uf&rsquo;ritah sif&rsquo;vasayh</em></span>, as above. And then it is money of significance for all matters. And one who robs his fellow of such money, or denies [owing] his fellow such money &mdash; that is, the measure of a <span class="key-term">p&rsquo;rutah</span> &mdash; then he blemishes the <span class="key-term">emunah</span>. And then the court must concern itself with this case in order to rectify and elevate the <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, as above. Blessed is Hashem forever, <em>omain</em>.
</p>

<div class="ref-block">
<em>This teaching is repeated below [further in the text] in a slightly different formulation, and there it will be explained at length.</em>
</div>

