<!-- ====== TRANSLATOR SUMMARY ====== -->
<div class="translator-summary">
  <h3>Summary &mdash; From the Translator</h3>

  <p>
  This teaching develops the parallel between the role of the judge and the dynamics of prayer, both grounded in the concept of <em>dibbur emes</em> &mdash; speaking truth. Just as one who prays must speak truth so that G-d&rsquo;s light illuminates and reveals the way through the darkness of confusion, so too the judge must ensure that every word of his ruling is spoken in truth, without any inclination &mdash; and thereby the light of Hashem (which <em>is</em> truth) enters the courtroom and reveals who is actually telling the truth.
  </p>

  <p>
  The central Zoharic formula &mdash; &ldquo;<em>Tzedek</em>, when joined with <em>emes</em>, becomes <em>emunah</em>&rdquo; &mdash; is applied throughout: the judge&rsquo;s truthful speech (<em>emes</em>) rectifies the fallen faith (<em>emunah</em>/<em>tzedek</em>) that was blemished by the dishonest litigant. This is why judgment occurs during the day (the aspect of truth), why the judge is called &ldquo;<em>Elokim</em>&rdquo; (G-d&rsquo;s light is clothed within him), why true judges must be ordained in Eretz Yisroel (the land = emunah), and why even the smallest monetary unit &mdash; the <em>p&rsquo;rutah</em> &mdash; is significant enough to warrant judicial attention, since it alludes to the whispered prayer of the lips (the gateway of <em>emunah</em>).
  </p>

  <p class="translator-note">
    &mdash; Translator&rsquo;s summary for ajew.org
  </p>
</div>

